1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,304
Wednesday, Bekzod has an idea.

2
00:00:02,354 --> 00:00:08,472
Malika's shop could keep each buyer's card number in its orders table, just in case a refund ever needs it.

3
00:00:08,522 --> 00:00:14,952
In Lesson 3, the card number lived only in the card zone, and everything else got a mask or a token.

4
00:00:15,002 --> 00:00:20,376
This lesson shows why that rule exists, and how one locked room keeps its cost small.

5
00:00:20,426 --> 00:00:22,728
Bekzod's idea seems harmless.

6
00:00:22,778 --> 00:00:24,600
Then the numbers spread.

7
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:27,912
A report reads the orders table every morning.

8
00:00:27,962 --> 00:00:30,144
A backup copies it every night.

9
00:00:30,194 --> 00:00:32,928
Bekzod's laptop holds a copy for testing.

10
00:00:32,978 --> 00:00:37,560
Now each of those places holds card numbers too, so each of them must be checked.

11
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:41,352
One lost laptop can leak every card the shop has ever seen.

12
00:00:41,402 --> 00:00:43,320
Those checks come from one rulebook.

13
00:00:43,370 --> 00:00:47,400
PCI DSS is the card industry's security rulebook.

14
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:51,696
It checks every system that stores, handles or sends a card number.

15
00:00:51,746 --> 00:00:56,880
It also checks every system connected to those, or able to change how safe they are.

16
00:00:56,930 --> 00:01:01,872
The check covers the system's network, its logins, its logs and its changes.

17
00:01:01,922 --> 00:01:06,216
So the report, the backup and Bekzod's laptop are all in the check now.

18
00:01:06,266 --> 00:01:11,280
The more places a card number can reach, the bigger the check, and the more ways to fail it.

19
00:01:11,330 --> 00:01:13,560
Most teams try to pass a big check.

20
00:01:13,610 --> 00:01:16,704
I think the better move is to shrink what gets checked.

21
00:01:16,754 --> 00:01:18,888
Put card numbers in one locked room.

22
00:01:18,938 --> 00:01:23,016
Give every other system a card token instead, never the number.

23
00:01:23,066 --> 00:01:28,248
In this system, that room is the card zone, and Timur runs it at the bank.

24
00:01:28,298 --> 00:01:32,304
It has its own database, its own keys and its own logins.

25
00:01:32,354 --> 00:01:34,296
It shares none of them with ecom.

26
00:01:34,346 --> 00:01:36,168
It has only two doors.

27
00:01:36,218 --> 00:01:39,552
One door is for buyers' browsers: the card form.

28
00:01:39,602 --> 00:01:43,104
The other door is for partner systems, like ecom.

29
00:01:43,154 --> 00:01:45,792
The database inside has no door at all.

30
00:01:45,842 --> 00:01:49,872
The room has no screen of its own, so follow one payment through it.

31
00:01:49,922 --> 00:01:54,216
A buyer opens the bank's payment page to pay for Malika's roses.

32
00:01:54,266 --> 00:01:59,256
The card form under "Card details" is the first door, opened inside the page.

33
00:01:59,306 --> 00:02:03,408
The buyer types the card, and it travels straight through that door into the room.

34
00:02:03,458 --> 00:02:05,352
ecom's servers are not on the way.

35
00:02:05,402 --> 00:02:09,144
Inside, the card is locked away, and a token comes back out.

36
00:02:09,194 --> 00:02:15,672
Then ecom knocks on the second door: it asks the room to charge the card, and it names the card only by its token.

37
00:02:15,722 --> 00:02:17,544
Timur looks at the running card zone.

38
00:02:17,594 --> 00:02:19,440
Two doors answer from outside.

39
00:02:19,490 --> 00:02:22,560
The database answers to nobody outside the room.

40
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:26,976
And in the bank admin portal, Dilnoza sees only masked numbers.

41
00:02:27,026 --> 00:02:29,856
ecom holds tokens, never card numbers.

42
00:02:29,906 --> 00:02:31,728
Now the honest part.

43
00:02:31,778 --> 00:02:33,600
ecom still gets a check.

44
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:38,136
It shows the card form on its page, and it uses tokens to take payments.

45
00:02:38,186 --> 00:02:42,048
So it is connected to the room: its check is smaller, but not none.

46
00:02:42,098 --> 00:02:44,088
And the room needs a building.

47
00:02:44,138 --> 00:02:47,904
The card zone runs inside the bank's own secured network.

48
00:02:47,954 --> 00:02:51,504
The bank's firewalls, guards and monitoring stand around it.

49
00:02:51,554 --> 00:02:59,112
The card zone fits in through its two narrow doors, and it shares no parts with the bank's other systems, so it breaks nothing around it.

50
00:02:59,162 --> 00:03:02,040
The product claims no certificate for itself.

51
00:03:02,090 --> 00:03:05,520
The bank's own assessor checks the room and the building together.

52
00:03:05,570 --> 00:03:08,544
So a leak from ecom leaks no card number at all.

53
00:03:08,594 --> 00:03:15,360
The buyer's card stays safe, ecom's check stays small, and the bank guards one small room instead of everything.

54
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:17,232
Bekzod drops his idea.

55
00:03:17,282 --> 00:03:19,944
A refund needs the payment, not the card.

56
00:03:19,994 --> 00:03:24,048
The product delivers the locked room, and the bank owns the building.

57
00:03:24,098 --> 00:03:27,960
Next: how ecom takes a payment without ever seeing the card.
